{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'output|result|stream|repl|VM|vm|loss|gap' docs/design/yin-repl-design.md | head -n 100\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'output|result|stream|repl|VM|vm|loss|gap' docs/design/yin-repl-design.md | head -n 100\"","aggregated_output":"3:> **Status (2026-09-16):** this describes v1 `yin.repl`, deleted by\n4:> `yin.vm.v1-retirement.implementation-plan.md` U6. The live REPL is\n5:> `yin.repl`; see `yin.repl.implementation-plan.md`.\n9:The **Yin REPL** is an interactive command-line shell for the Yin VM ecosystem. It operates at the **datom level**, treating everything as data. Users can:\n11:1. Submit raw datom streams `[[e a v t m] ...]` directly to a VM for execution\n14:4. Execute shell commands as ordinary function calls: `(vm :ast-walker)`, `(telemetry)`, `(quit)`\n18:**Supported platforms:** JVM (Clojure), Node.js (ClojureScript), ClojureDart  \n19:**Branch:** `repl`\n25:**Everything is data.** The shell reads Clojure/Python/PHP source code and translates it to a universal AST, which becomes a datom stream. The VM executes datoms. Users see all three representations and can work at the level they need.\n27:**Explicit execution models.** VMs are not hidden. Users can inspect and control which VM is active, watch telemetry, and connect remotely over WebSockets.\n29:**Functions as commands.** Shell commands (`vm`, `lang`, `compile`, `quit`, etc.) are not special syntax. They are ordinary Clojure function calls that the shell intercepts and dispatches before evaluating.\n36:src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc           — Portable core (all business logic)\n37:src/clj/yin/repl_main.clj        — JVM entry point (stdin I/O)\n38:src/cljs/yin/repl_main.cljs      — Node.js entry point (readline I/O)\n39:src/cljd/yin/repl_main.cljd      — ClojureDart entry point (dart:io)\n40:test/yin/repl_test.cljc          — Portable test suite\n43:The **portable core** (`src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc`) contains all shell logic:\n45:- VM lifecycle management\n48:- Telemetry streaming\n52:- Reading input lines (stdin on JVM, readline on Node.js, dart:io on Dart)\n53:- Printing output\n64:{:vm-type         :ast-walker            ; VM backend (ast-walker is the only one; yin.vm-consumers.implementation-plan.md deleted the rest)\n66: :vm              <vm-instance>          ; Current VM instance (store persists across evals)\n67: :telemetry-stream nil | <dao.stream>    ; Stream for VM telemetry datoms\n68: :remote-stream   nil | <dao.stream>}    ; WebSocket connection to remote shell\n71:Each eval preserves `:vm` state (its `:store` accumulates `yin/def` bindings), creating a natural REPL session.\n79:1. **Shell commands** — function calls with head `vm`, `lang`, `compile`, `reset`, `connect`, `disconnect`, `telemetry`, `help`, `quit`\n80:   - Example: `(vm :ast-walker)` → resets to a fresh ASTWalkerVM\n84:2. **Remote delegation** — if `:remote-stream` is set, forward the input string to the remote shell via WebSocket\n85:   - Remote shell evaluates and returns result\n87:3. **Raw datom stream** — if input is `[[e a v t m] ...]`\n88:   - Directly execute via `vm/load-program` + `vm/run`\n91:   - Directly execute via `vm/eval`\n94:   - Compile via Yang compiler → AST → `vm/eval`\n100:### `eval-input [state input-str]` → `[new-state result-str]`\n104:### `eval-datoms [state datoms]` → `[new-state result-str]`\n106:Execute a raw datom stream. Calls `vm/load-program` with `{:node root-id :datoms datoms}` and `vm/run`.\n108:### `eval-ast [state ast-map]` → `[new-state result-str]`\n110:Execute an AST map. Calls `vm/eval` which internally translates AST → datoms.\n125:| `(vm :ast-walker)` | Reset to a fresh ASTWalkerVM (warns about lost store) |\n126:| `(lang :python)` | Switch input language to Python (no VM change) |\n128:| `(reset)` | Create fresh VM of same type |\n129:| `(connect \"daostream:ws://host:port\")` | Connect to remote shell; forward evals |\n132:| `(telemetry \"daostream:ws://host:port\")` | Stream telemetry to remote WebSocket |\n136:### `make-vm [vm-type telemetry-stream]` → `<vm>`\n138:Create a fresh VM instance. If `telemetry-stream` is non-nil, wire the VM to emit `[e a v t m]` datoms to that stream on every step, park, resume, halt, and effect.\n142:Start telemetry streaming.\n143:- `sink = :stderr` — background loop reads datoms from telemetry stream, pretty-prints to stderr\n144:- `sink = \"daostream:ws://...\"` — relay datoms to remote WebSocket\n148:Expose the shell as a WebSocket server. Accept remote clients and dispatch `:op/eval`, `:op/vm`, `:op/lang`, `:op/reset` requests via `dao.stream.apply` protocol.\n154:### `(vm :ast-walker)`\n156:`:ast-walker` is the only supported VM backend; the others\n158:`yin.vm-consumers.implementation-plan.md`. Warns that `:store` state is\n159:lost and creates a fresh VM.\n162:yin> (vm :ast-walker)\n163:Switched to ASTWalkerVM (store cleared)\n172:Switch the input language for source code compilation. Does not affect the current VM.\n207:Reset the current VM to a fresh state.\n215:ASTWalkerVM reset\n220:### `(connect \"daostream:ws://remote-host:port\")`\n225:yin> (connect \"daostream:ws://localhost:7777\")\n245:### `(telemetry)` and `(telemetry \"daostream:ws://...\")`\n247:Enable or disable VM telemetry. Telemetry emits `[e a v t m]` datom snapshots of the VM's CESK state at each step, park, resume, halt, and effect.\n255:[root-id :vm/type :vm/snapshot 0 0]\n256:[root-id :vm/model :ast-walker 0 0]\n257:[root-id :vm/phase :step 0 0]\n260:yin> (telemetry \"daostream:ws://monitor:9000\")\n338:clj -M:repl --port 7777 --headless\n341:Remote clients connect and send requests via `dao.stream.apply`:\n344:{:dao.stream.apply/id   \"req-123\"\n345: :dao.stream.apply/op   :op/eval\n346: :dao.stream.apply/args [\"(+ 1 2)\"]}\n352:{:dao.stream.apply/id    \"req-123\"\n353: :dao.stream.apply/value \"3\"}\n360:When telemetry is enabled, the VM emits `[e a v t m]` datom snapshots to a `dao.stream`. Each snapshot captures the full CESK state:\n363:[root-id :vm/type       :vm/snapshot 0 0]\n364:[root-id :vm/vm-id      :ast-walker-vm-1 0 0]\n365:[root-id :vm/model      :ast-walker 0 0]\n366:[root-id :vm/step       123 0 0]\n367:[root-id :vm/phase      :step 0 0]\n368:[root-id :vm/blocked?   false 0 0]\n369:[root-id :vm/halted?    false 0 0]\n370:[root-id :vm/control    control-summary-eid 0 0]\n371:[root-id :vm/env        env-summary-eid 0 0]\n372:[root-id :vm/store      store-summary-eid 0 0]\n373:[root-id :vm/k          k-summary-eid 0 0]\n374:[root-id :vm/value      result-value 0 0]\n392:### VM State Persistence\n394:Each `eval` preserves the `:vm` state. The VM's `:store` field is mutable in the sense that `vm/eval` returns an updated VM with accumulated bindings. By threading the VM through evals, the shell naturally implements a REPL session.\n402:When `:remote-stream` is set, the shell becomes a thin client. It forwards raw input strings to the remote over WebSocket. The remote parses, compiles, and evals, returning the result string. This preserves the remote's `:store` state transparently.\n406:Telemetry and normal eval are concurrent. A background loop reads from `:telemetry-stream` and either prints or forwards. The VM's emissions don't block normal eval.\n412:### JVM\n415:clj -M:repl [FLAGS]\n420:  --telemetry-stream <url>     Stream telemetry to remote WebSocket\n427:npx shadow-cljs run yin.repl/-main [FLAGS]\n429:(Same flags as JVM)\n436:dart run bin/yin_repl_main.dart [FLAGS]\n438:(Same flags as JVM)\n448:clj -M:test -n yin.repl-test\n454:- VM switching\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
